  "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ is $t\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242901/assets/a55ef2c927b26c9beba9b44f669c817cf749b79de163b4bf0505d15c6ec31630.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-1,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$",
